Chapter 4 — Part 1
The net café sat two streets over, sign half-lit, windows fogged from the machines inside. They walked the distance without hurrying. Hina’s bag bumped her hip. Curtis kept the duffel on the shoulder that didn’t pull the tape on his ribs.
“Can I ask?” she said after a block. Careful. “Your quirk. You don’t have to get specific. I just… U.A. hero course. People are going to wonder.”
Curtis watched the crosswalk. “Enhanced Physiology. That’s what’s on the paper.” He didn’t dress it up. “Body came out stronger than most. Denser. Faster recovery. I trained it hard because for a long time everybody thought I was quirkless. Turns out the floor was just higher than they tested for. I can push past it when I need to. Don’t always need to.”
Hina nodded, taking it in. The low pull of Free Love still sat warm between them; it made her notice the way his voice stayed steady more than the words themselves. She didn’t fight the noticing. “So it’s always on. Not something you flip.”
“Baseline.” He glanced at her. “What about yours?”
She huffed a small laugh, surprised he’d turned it around. “Nothing that gets you into U.A. Minor charm-type. Makes people a little more likely to take a flyer if I’m the one holding it. Fades fast. Doesn’t work on anybody who’s already decided to keep walking.” Her fingers tightened on the bag strap. “Useful for the job I have. Not much else.”
“Useful’s useful.” Curtis’s tone didn’t change. “You still handed me one and I stopped. So it did its work.”
Hina’s cheeks warmed again. She looked ahead so he wouldn’t see how long the smile stayed. “Maybe. Or you were just looking for hours.”
“Both can be true.”
They reached the café door. Neon buzzed. Inside, rows of booths glowed under privacy screens. Hina paused with her hand on the frame, then pushed it open and held it.
“Booths in the back are quieter if you actually want to sleep. Tell them you’re with the Lumen referral. They give the overnight rate.”
Curtis stepped past her into the cooled air and the low whir of machines. “Appreciate the walk.”
“Anytime,” she said, and meant it more than the word usually carried. She followed him in, bag light on her shoulder, already wondering whether he’d still be in the same booth when her own shift ended tomorrow.
The net café smelled like cold air and instant coffee. Rows of high-backed booths ran toward the back, most of them dark behind privacy screens. The clerk at the counter glanced up, took in Hina’s Lumen bag, and didn’t ask questions. Overnight rate. One booth. Curtis paid cash. The clerk slid a keycard and a thin blanket across the counter without looking twice.
Hina walked him past the brighter stations near the front. A couple of night-shift regulars had headphones on. No one stared long. She stopped at a booth three from the end, the screen already dimmed, the chair wide enough to sleep in if you tilted it back.
“This one’s quiet,” she said. “Wall on one side. They don’t care if you stay till morning as long as the card’s paid.”
Curtis set the duffel inside and tested the chair. It reclined clean. “Works.”
She lingered in the narrow gap between booths. The Free Love warmth still sat low and steady; it made the small space feel closer than it was. Her fingers rested on the edge of the partition. “You don’t have to decide about the club tonight. Or the upstairs thing. Janice won’t push. Tatsuya won’t either.”
“I know.” He looked at her. “You walking back alone?”
“Train’s still running. I’ll be fine.” The answer came easy. She didn’t move yet. “If you take another shift, I’ll probably be on flyers again. Or around. Just so you know the face.”
Curtis nodded once. Practical. “I’ll look for it.”
Hina’s mouth curved, small. She stepped back so he could pull the screen most of the way shut. “Get some sleep, Curtis. Classes tomorrow.”
“You too.”
She hesitated one second longer, then turned and headed for the front. The low pull in his chest didn’t change when she left. He still didn’t have a name for it. He locked the booth from the inside, kicked the chair back, and set an alarm for early enough to make the first train toward U.A. The uniform in the duffel was clean. The city kept moving outside the fogged windows. He closed his eyes and let the machines hum.
The booth screen stayed dark after Hina left. Curtis waited until her footsteps faded, then unzipped the side pocket of the duffel and set the mask on the narrow table.
Matte black. Angular plating. Red optical slits that caught the low café light even powered down. White technical markings ran along the jaw and temple—F3RC, caution stamps, a small triangle near the crown. He turned it once, checked the seal at the neck joint, and thumbed a recessed panel until it clicked. A thin USB slid free from the housing. He plugged it into the booth’s port.
The fans spun up. A second later the speakers gave a soft, cultured chime.
“Good evening, sir.” The voice was older, precise, the kind of British cadence that sounded like it had never raised itself in its life. “My systems read we are in what you call a net café. Privacy screens are adequate. External monitoring is minimal.”
Curtis leaned back in the chair. “Good evening, Sir Jeeves. Yes. It appears we are.” He kept his voice low. “Tonight I need you to scan low-crime corridors in the area. We’ll head out tomorrow night. Hopefully I’ll have an apartment sorted by then. If the job doesn’t eat the hours.”
A brief pause. “Work, sir?”
“Host club. Trial shift tonight went fine. They offered nights. Manager and floor lead seem straight about boundaries. Could cover rent while classes run.” Curtis watched the mask’s optical slits flicker once as Jeeves parsed the booth’s connection. “Name’s Lumen. Near the station.”
Jeeves made a sound that was almost a laugh—dry, polite, faintly horrified. “Good heavens, sir. The manner in which you handle villains is not, I fear, directly transferable to the handling of women in a commercial social setting. One typically does not resolve awkward table conversation with a headbutt.”
Curtis’s mouth twitched. “I think I can manage. I think.”
“Optimism is noted and filed under ‘pending observation,’ sir.” The mask’s red slits brightened a fraction, data scrolling too fast to read across the inner display before it dimmed again. “Low-crime routes are already mapping. Three viable corridors within four kilometers. I will have the cleanest one queued by the time you wake. Do try not to acquire additional employment complications before then.”
“Noted.” Curtis unplugged the USB, slid it home, and set the mask back into the duffel. The booth hummed. Outside, the city kept its nighttime noise. He tilted the chair the rest of the way and closed his eyes, alarm already set.
Jeeves stayed quiet in the housing. The low, unnamed warmth in Curtis’s chest stayed exactly where it always was.
